{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"The working directory is a scratchpad for the \"babylon-lite-minecraft-fps\" project. The user asked, \"What's different?\" about Babylon-Lite and Babylon.js. I've got the overall picture from the reference repository. So now I'll write a concise answer.\n\nKey differences:\n\n1. **Positioning / goals**: Babylon.js is a mature, broad engine (WebGL1/2 + WebGPU, wide feature set, GUI, XR, tools ecosystem). Babylon-Lite is a WebGPU-only, tree-shakable reimplementation that aims for pixel-identical output at a fraction of the bundle size. Lite is not a compatibility layer or a replacement; the two are developed in parallel.\n\n2. **API shape**: BJS uses an OOP class API with register*() side effects at import time. Lite uses plain-data interfaces + standalone functions (one-way data ownership: the scene owns components, components don't reference the scene), zero module-level side effects, materials own their shaders, no raw GPU handles in the public API. Factory functions take no scene parameter; you use addToScene().\n\n3. **Graphics**: Lite is WebGPU-only, WGSL shaders, render pipelines / bind groups / compute; BJS supports WebGL 1/2 + WebGPU, GLSL/HLSL.\n\n4. **Feature coverage**: Lite covers the core rendering (PBR, standard, NME subset, glTF with many extensions, IBL, shadows including CSM, skeletal animation, morphs, VAT, picking, Gaussian splatting, physics Havok V2 subset, navigation Recast). What's missing from Lite: particles, GUI, WebXR, LOD, decals, SSAO/SSR, area lights, point light shadows, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, procedural / video / dynamic textures, asset manager, serialization, follow / universal / VR cameras, etc.\n\n5. **Tooling / monorepo structure**: BJS is a lerna monorepo with dev/public/tools packages (core, materials, gui, loaders, playground, editor, MCP server, etc.). Lite is a pnpm workspace with three packages: babylon-lite (engine), @babylonjs/lite-compat (a BJS-shape compatibility layer with vite/rollup/webpack/esbuild shims for migration), babylon-lite-gl (empty/placeholder). Plus lab (Vite scene gallery), tests (vitest unit + Playwright parity/perf tests), reference golden screenshots, and CI that enforces bundle-size ceilings.\n\n6. **Test / parity infrastructure**: Lite uses pixel-diff parity tests against BJS golden references, bundle-size ceiling tests, and RAF performance benchmarks — BJS has its own test suite.\n\n7. **Philosophy**: Size and performance take priority over simplicity / compatibility; strict rules in GUIDANCE.md (immutable): no module-level side effects, lazy caches, features as dynamically-imported extension modules, WGSL minification, one-way data ownership, pure state interfaces, @internal tag stripping in d.ts.\n\n8. **Lite-specific features (★)**: ObservableVec3, high-precision / floating-origin matrices (eye-relative), frame-graph render system, GPU frame timer, thin instances instead of InstancedMesh.\n\nKeep the answer reasonably concise, but complete. Name files: docs/lite/02-feature-comparison.md, philosophy.md, GUIDANCE.md, packages/babylon-lite, @babylonjs/lite-compat.\n\nFormat: short sections, with bullet points if needed. The user is a technical person. Concise paragraphs + a few bullet points.\n","seq":932,"t":95681,"text":"\n\nI've read through the reference. Here's what actually differs, based on the source:\n\n**Positioning** (`philosophy.md`, `docs/lite/02-feature-comparison.md`)\n- **Babylon.js**: mature, broad engine — WebGL 1/2 *and* WebGPU, GUI, XR, decals, particles, asset manager, serialization, plus a huge tools ecosystem (playground, editors, MCP servers) in `packages/tools/`.\n- **Babylon-Lite**: WebGPU-*exclusive*, tree-shakable reimplementation targeting pixel-identical output at a fraction of the bundle size. It explicitly is *not* a replacement — it prioritizes size > performance > simplicity, and backward compatibility is not a goal.\n\n**API shape** — the biggest practical difference:\n- BJS is OOP: `new Mesh(name, ...)`, `scene.add()`, `register*()` calls at import time, classes with methods.\n- Lite uses **plain-data interfaces + standalone functions** (`GUIDANCE.md` \"One-Way Data Ownership\" / \"Pure State Interfaces\"): `createHemisphericLight()` takes no scene; you `addToScene(scene, light)`. Zero module-level side effects (no `register*()`, no module-level `Map()` — kills tree-shaking), materials own their WGSL shaders/pipelines, and no raw `GPUTexture`/`GPUBuffer` handles in the public API.\n\n**Graphics core**: Lite is WGSL + render pipelines/bind groups/compute only; BJS has GLSL/HLSL across WebGL/WebGPU. Lite adds a frame-graph render system, GPU frame timer, and eye-relative floating-origin matrices (a ★ Lite advantage per the comparison doc).\n\n**Feature gaps** (from the comparison table): Lite has no particles, GUI, WebXR, LOD, SSAO/SSR, decals, area lights, point-light shadows, OBJ/STL/FBX loaders, procedural/video/dynamic textures, or the classic `InstancedMesh` API (use thin instances instead). It covers the core: PBR + Standard + a large NME subset, glTF with ~25 extensions, IBL, ESM/PCF/CSM shadows, skeletal animation/morphs/VAT, GPU picking, Gaussian splatting, Havok V2 subset, Recast navigation.\n\n**Repo structure**: BJS is a lerna monorepo (`dev/`, `public/`, `tools/` with 30+ packages). Lite is a pnpm workspace with just three packages: `babylon-lite` (engine), `@babylonjs/lite-compat` (a BJS-shaped migration layer with vite/rollup/webpack/esbuild shims), and `babylon-lite-gl` (currently empty) — plus `lab/` (Vite scene gallery), `tests/lite/` (vitest unit + Playwright plumbing/parity/perf), and `reference/lite/` golden screenshots. Its CI enforces runtime bundle-size ceilings and pixel-diff parity against BJS goldens, which BJS doesn't do.\n\n**Workflow**: Lite enforces immutable rules in `GUIDANCE.md` — every optional feature must be a dynamically-imported extension module (glTF ext, PBR fragment ext), lazy caches only, WGSL minification in prod builds, and `@internal` tags stripped from emitted d.ts.\n\nWant me to go deeper on any piece — e.g., the compat layer's shims or the frame-graph renderer?","timings":{"cache_n":14848,"draft_n":3626,"draft_n_accepted":972,"predicted_ms":19540.0,"predicted_n":1490,"prompt_ms":730.0,"prompt_n":478},"to_request":410}
