{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"All the necessary API pieces are in place:\n\n- `createMeshFromData(engine, name, positions, normals, indices)` — for terrain chunks\n- `updateMeshPositions(engine, mesh, positions)` — zero-allocation in-place position update (perfect for crater deformation — vertex count doesn't change!)\n- `updateMeshNormals(engine, mesh, normals)` — in-place normal update\n- `computeNormals(positions, indices)` — CPU normal computation\n- `onBeforeRender(scene, cb(deltaMs))` — game loop\n- `createFreeCamera` + set `scene.camera`\n- `createStandardMaterial`, `createBox`, `createEsmDirectionalShadowGenerator`, `setShadowTaskCasterMeshes`, `registerSceneWithShadowSupport`\n- `setFog(scene, {mode, density, start, end, color})`\n- `removeFromScene(scene, mesh)` — for destroyed crates\n- `scene.clearColor` — sky color\n\nWith `updateMeshPositions` + `updateMeshNormals`, chunk reconstruction is cheap (no reallocation).\n\nImportant: `updateMeshPositions` doesn't update the CPU picking geometry — but since we're doing heightfield raycasts on the CPU ourselves, the CPU data isn't needed.\n\nShadows: the ESM shadow generator renders casters. When the terrain deforms, the shadow map needs updating — does the shadow task re-render every frame, or only when dirty? ESM has `forceRefreshEveryFrame`. The simplest thing is: set `forceRefreshEveryFrame: true` on the shadow generator? It might have a performance cost, but for this demo it's fine. Actually, let me check how shadow generators decide to refresh… there might be a \"dirty\" flag. Quick check of shadow-base. If it's complex, just enable forceRefreshEveryFrame.\n\nCrate: a box that gets destroyed on hit → `removeFromScene`. Crates should also cast shadows — `setShadowTaskCasterMeshes(generator, [...])` — after removing a crate, the list still references it. Is there a function to update casters? `setShadowTaskCasterMeshes` can be called again with the remaining crates. No problem.\n\nNext, project setup. Working directory: `/private/tmp/.../scratchpad/run3/babylon-lite-minecraft-fps` (empty).\n\nPlan:\n1. `package.json` — name it babylon-lite-quake-fps? The directory name is babylon-lite-minecraft-fps, but the user asked for Quake-style. Keep the directory, and set the name in package.json to something like \"quake-lite\". Dependencies: `babylon-lite` — linked to the reference repo's package. In pnpm, `pnpm add link:~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite`? The reference repo uses pnpm. However, the reference repo's package.json main is `./src/index.ts` — meaning Vite will directly compile TS source from the linked package. That's how the lab does it (workspace). Create a standalone Vite project that depends on the local package via file: link.\n\nNote: babylon-lite's package.json has `main: ./src/index.ts` and `exports` maps to src TS files, `sideEffects: false`. Vite can handle TS inside linked dependencies (it's ESM TS). The lab does exactly this via the pnpm workspace. Use `file:` protocol with pnpm.\n\nActually simpler: `pnpm add ../path` doesn't work outside a workspace; use `file:~/git/reference/Babylon/Babylon-Lite/packages/babylon-lite`. pnpm creates a symlink (file: protocol → symlink). Vite will process TS from node_modules symlink… Vite by default doesn't transform TS in node_modules unless it's a linked dependency — Vite detects linked packages (symlinks) and excludes them from the node_modules optimization, processing them as source. Yes, Vite handles this: \"linked dependencies are not pre-bundled\" and go through the transform pipeline. The lab's vite config is probably just defaults. Should work.\n\n2. `vite.config.ts` — dev server on port 5180 (per system note: dev server uses port 5180, 5173 is in use). Set `server: { port: 5180 }`.\n\n3. `index.html` — canvas + HUD overlay (crosshair, health, ammo, message).\n\n4. `src/main.ts` — engine setup:\n   - createEngine(canvas)\n   - createSceneContext(engine)\n   - scene.clearColor = dark sky (e.g., [0.02, 0.02, 0.05])\n   - setFog(scene, { mode: 1 (exp), density ~0.012, color dark })\n   - Terrain: build heightfield, chunked meshes\n   - Crates: several boxes\n   - Light: directional + ESM shadow generator (casters: crates; terrain receives shadows)\n   - Camera: FreeCamera at spawn position\n   - Controls: pointer lock, WASD, jump, shoot\n   - onBeforeRender: player physics, camera update\n   - registerSceneWithShadowSupport, startEngine\n\n5. `src/terrain.ts` — heightfield generation + chunk mesh + crater + height sampling + raycast.\n\n6. `src/player.ts` — movement/physics.\n\n7. `src/weapon.ts` — hitscan, ray vs heightfield + crates, damage.\n\n8. `src/controls.ts` — pointer lock + keyboard.\n\nHeightfield details:\n- World size: 100×100 units, centered at origin. Grid: 128×128 cells → 129×129 vertices. Cell size = 100/128 = 0.78125.\n- Chunks: 16 cells × 16 cells → 8×8 = 64 chunks. Chunk vertices: 17×17 = 289, indices: 16*16*2 = 512 triangles.\n- Heights: base flat 0, plus a few Gaussian hills (Quake arenas are mostly flat with some variation), value noise via hash-based smoothing. Keep amplitudes small (±2).\n- Player spawns at center where terrain is flat.\n\nCrater:\n- `carveCrater(worldX, worldZ, radius=3, depth=2.5)`: iterate grid vertices within radius (with margin), for each vertex: d = distance; s = smoothstep(radius*0.4, radius, d); floor = hCenter - depth… per-vertex compute: target = mix(hAtHit - depth, h, s); h = min(h, target). Track affected chunks, then for each affected chunk rebuild that chunk's positions/normals arrays from the height grid, and call updateMeshPositions + updateMeshNormals.\n\nNormal computation: `computeNormals(positions, indices)` — but per chunk, we need a local positions array and indices with local vertex IDs. Keep a per-chunk index template (Uint32Array, static) and a per-chunk vertex index mapping: global vertex id = ((z0 + iz) * (N+1)) + (x0 + ix).\n\nRay vs heightfield: march the ray in steps; use grid-based DDA for efficiency. Simpler and more robust approach: step along the ray with adaptive step (0.25), and at each point sample the height bilinearly; if the point's y < height → hit, refine with bisection between the last safe point and the hit point. Max range 150. This is accurate enough and simple.\n\nRay vs crate: ray-AABB (slab method) per live crate; take the nearest hit.\n\nHit effects:\n- Crater on terrain hit\n- Destroy crate (removeFromScene + splice from list + update shadow casters)\n- Hit marker on HUD + muzzle flash (brief point light? Light addition needs re-registration? Adding a light after registerScene — the doc's lifecycle note says added-after-register won't render. Safer: pre-create a point light for the muzzle flash at the camera position, and toggle its intensity. Does PointLight exist? Per the feature table, \"Point Light ✅\". Let me quickly check the createPointLight export. Pre-create with intensity 0, set intensity=1 on shot, decay in the loop.\n\nSound: skip (no audio API in Lite — KHR_audio isn't supported).\n\nHUD: crosshair div, health (take damage? no enemies… maybe skip damage), ammo counter (reload R, 8-shot clip), shot counter / \"craters\" counter, FPS display, draw calls (engine.drawCallCount), controls help. Quake style: yellow text, monospace font.\n\nWeapon view model: a simple box in front of the camera (child of the camera — FreeCamera has children; parented nodes get the push invalidation). The feature table says \"Gizmos ✅ … on a utility layer\". Parenting a box to the camera should work (children + worldMatrix). Create a box, scale it, parent it to the camera (set `box.parent = cam`? For Mesh, parent is IWorldMatrixProvider). Position it at camera-relative [0.3, -0.3, -0.8]. Add a kick animation on fire. This gives it a Quake feel. Risk: child world matrix composition — FreeCamera's worldMatrix includes position; child TRS is local. Should work.\n\nRecoil + view bob: small additions, keep minimal but juicy: camera pitch kick that decays, weapon kick.\n\nHealth/ammo: ammo 20, reload 1.2s, infinite reserves. Health 100, no damage source… make falling damage? Keep it simple: no health, show \"CRATERS\" count and FPS. Actually, a small self-damage on… no. Keep HUD: AMMO, CRATERS, FPS, DRAW CALLS, controls help.\n\nPhysics: gravity -30, jump velocity 10, walk speed 12, mouse sensitivity. Ground: sampleHeight(x,z) + eye height 1.6. Map boundary: clamp position to [-48, 48] with soft walls.\n\nAlso `fixedDeltaMs` — scene has a fixedDeltaMs field; onBeforeRender cb receives deltaMs. Use deltaMs with a clamp.\n\nNow — one concern: `registerSceneWithShadowSupport` — does the ESM generator need casters set before registration? scene18 sets shadowGenerator on the light, calls setShadowTaskCasterMeshes, then registers. Follow that order.\n\nTerrain receiveShadows = true.\n\nOne more check: the signature of `createStandardMaterial()` — from scene18: `createStandardMaterial()` returns a material with `diffuseColor`, `specularColor`, `emissiveColor`, `diffuseTexture` properties. Good.\n\nCheck the exports for `createPointLight` + `createDirectionalLight` + `removeFromScene`. Also check the signature of `setShadowTaskCasterMeshes`.\n","seq":3605,"t":258478,"text":"\n\nI have the full API picture now. Let me verify a few remaining exports, then scaffold the project.\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":37632,"draft_n":7182,"draft_n_accepted":1634,"predicted_ms":44590.0,"predicted_n":2660,"prompt_ms":1940.0,"prompt_n":1506},"to_request":2619}
