{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"still being ignored. Hmm. pnpm 11: maybe `onlyBuiltDependencies` inside pnpm-workspace.yaml is correct, but the install state has already been recorded... let me check the pnpm docs: in pnpm 10, `pnpm.onlyBuiltDependencies` in package.json was the method; in pnpm 11 the settings moved to pnpm-workspace.yaml. The warning earlier said \"The 'pnpm' field in package.json is no longer read by pnpm... See https://pnpm.io/settings for the new home of each setting\". So pnpm-workspace.yaml should be correct. But it's still being ignored — maybe the install was cached and no rebuild was triggered. Let me run `pnpm rebuild` or check with `pnpm config get onlyBuiltDependencies`.\n\nActually, there's an even simpler approach: just run esbuild's postinstall manually: `node node_modules/.pnpm/esbuild@0.25.12/node_modules/esbuild/install.js`. Or just check whether the esbuild binary actually works — esbuild's postinstall just does verification/download of the platform-specific binary, and the binary is already included in the package (the @esbuild/darwin-arm64 optional dependency). esbuild's JS wrapper will find the binary via the optional dependency even without postinstall. In fact, esbuild's install.js runs `node install.js` to copy the binary, but the JS wrapper first checks for the binary at require time: if it can't find it, it runs the install script. In recent esbuild versions, the binary is provided by the @esbuild/darwin-arm64 package, and esbuild's lib finds it via require('@esbuild/darwin-arm64') — it works even without postinstall.\n\nLet me check whether vite can transform TS (that's what matters).\n","seq":9621,"t":693233,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":78080,"draft_n":1176,"draft_n_accepted":408,"predicted_ms":8790.0,"predicted_n":576,"prompt_ms":1600.0,"prompt_n":720},"to_request":9482}
