{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"In other words, `node_modules/babylon-lite` is a symlink to `.pnpm/babylon-lite@file+.../node_modules/babylon-lite`, and the .pnpm entry is a copy (not a symlink to the source). That's why Vite's optimizer treats it as a regular package (its realpath is inside node_modules), and esbuild's pre-bundling fails on `.js` → `.ts`.\n\nTwo fixes are needed:\n1. Make Vite treat it as a linked/source package: add `optimizeDeps.exclude: [\"babylon-lite\"]`.\n2. `.js` → `.ts` resolution in the dev server: Vite's esbuild transform handles this — the lab proves it works when the package is truly linked. With exclude, Vite will serve the file from node_modules/.pnpm/.../src/index.ts through the normal transform pipeline. Does Vite resolve `./engine/gpu-task-timing.js` → `.ts`?\n\nVite's resolver: for TS files, Vite tries `.ts` when `.js` doesn't exist — yes, Vite supports this (\"TypeScript files can use .js extensions to import .ts files\" — vite's resolve plugin checks for the ts equivalent). Actually, I recall that Vite has `resolve.tryTsExtensions` or something similar, which handles exactly this in dev. Since the lab runs `babylon-lite` from the workspace (symlink to the real directory) without any special resolver config, it should behave the same via the normal pipeline for excluded packages.\n\nThe simplest and most robust approach: make node_modules/babylon-lite a symlink to the real source directory (replacing pnpm's copy). Then Vite will detect it as linked (realpath outside node_modules) and automatically exclude it from pre-bundling — exactly the same as the lab. And the engine's runtime dependencies (text-shaper, recast, manifold) are already installed in .pnpm by pnpm (from the file: dependency's dependency graph), so they'll resolve fine even from the real directory? No wait — module resolution from the real directory (~/git/reference/...) walks up that directory tree to find node_modules, not my project'