ck) to catch type/import errors.\n- Maybe run a pure Node-based smoke test of the game logic modules (terrain/player/weapon) without GPU? terrain.ts imports babylon-lite (createMeshFromData etc.)—which needs a GPU device at runtime, but the module itself should be importable in Node? The babylon-lite module might reference `navigator`/GPU at import time… probably not at import time (zero side effects by design). I could unit-test the heightfield math (sampleHeight, carveCrater, raycast) by extracting them… but they're entangled with the engine. Simpler: verify with `vite build` + careful code review. Plus, dev server logs will show runtime errors if the user opens the page.\n\nActually, wait—maybe I can verify rendering without playwright: the sandbox has no browser. Rely on the build + code review and note what the user should look at.\n\nCurrent plan:\n1. Find the worktree and move the project there (copy files, set up node_modules there, re-link the symlink, restart the dev server from the worktree location).\n2. Implement bots:\n   - `src/bots.ts`: bot entities (position, yaw/pitch, state machine: wander/pick target/attack). Bots shoot at the player or other bots? \"Bots first\"—simplest: bots roam the arena, and if the player is within range/LOS, they aim and fire (hitscan); player takes damage? I haven't added player health yet. Add health: bot bullets hit the player → damage; player dies → respawn. Bots also shoot terrain (craters) and crates.\n   - Player health HUD.\n   - Bots rendered as boxes (body + head?)—simple: capsule-ish boxes, distinct color per bot. 4 bots.\n   - Bot movement: pick a random target point, walk toward it (terrain height sample for ground snap), avoid falling into craters (just snap to ground; craters are shallow so no problem).\n   - Bot firing: aim at player position with some spread; hitscan ray: check player hit (ray vs player position sphere), then terrain, then crates. Damage player on hit.\n   - Player death: respawn at spawn point, brief invulnerability.\n3. Keep multiplayer in mind: put game state into plain objects, and frame the loop as \"sim step\"—mention that this structure ports to a server later. Don't build networking.\n4. Verify: `vite build` for compile; review; restart dev server from the worktree on port 5180.\n\nLet me first check the worktree directory.\n","seq":12522,"t":7912155,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":97792,"draft_n":3066,"draft_n_accepted":751,"predicted_ms":24720.0,"predicted_n":1189,"prompt_ms":1440.0,"prompt_n":456},"to_request":12094}
