{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Wait — I need to re-verify the correspondence between the engine's CreateGround winding and my grid. The engine's ground: \"bottomLeft, bottomRight, topRight\" followed by \"bottomLeft, bottomRight, topLeft\". In BJS's CreateGround, the grid is generated with x going from left to right, and z going from \"bottom\" to \"top\"… In my chunk: ix is the x direction (west→east), iz is the z direction (north→south, since z increases). My a=(ix,iz), b=(ix+1,iz), c=(ix,iz+1), d=(ix+1,iz+1).\n\nIn BJS's CreateGround (from the Babylon.js source):\n```\nfor (iz...) for (ix...) {\n  bottomLeft = iz*(w+1)+ix... \n```\nBJS's CreateGround actually does:\n```\nvar first = iz * (nbVerticesX + 1) + ix;\nvar second = first + 1;\nvar third = first + nbVerticesX + 1;\n...\nindices[ii++] = first;     // bottomLeft\nindices[ii++] = second;    // bottomRight\nindices[ii++] = third + 1; // topRight\nindices[ii++] = first;\nindices[ii++] = second;\nindices[ii++] = third;     // topLeft\n```\nHmm, this gives (a, b, d) followed by (a, b, c) — matches what I wrote. And in BJS, the ground faces up (+Y) — BJS's CreateGround generates an upward-facing ground (default sideOrientation is BACK… in BJS the ground faces up). Since BJS uses RH by default… but Lite is LH! Does the same winding face up in LH?\n\nCulling depends on the winding in screen space, which depends on the handedness of the view. Hmm, this is getting subtle. The decisive fact: Lite's own `createGround` uses this winding, and it renders as an upward-facing ground (scene18 shows the ground with a texture and it's visible). Lite's renderer culls with its standard settings (WebGPU default is CCW front face). Therefore, whatever (a,b,d),(a,b,c) is relative to the (x→right, z→down-screen?) coordinate mapping, it's consistent with the engine's normal computation ((p1-p2)×(p3-p2) gives upward normals for this winding, as I verified in the coordinate space where the ground is y-up)