{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Build passes. Now, a few final sanity checks that can be done without a browser:\n\n1. **Smoke test of pure logic**: I can extract the terrain math and test it in Node — but terrain.ts imports babylon-lite at the top (createMeshFromData, etc.). Importing babylon-lite in Node: index.ts re-exports a ton of modules; some might reference browser APIs at module scope... by design, zero side effects, so it should import fine in Node. But some modules might import `@webgpu/types`-dependent code — types only. Let me try: a simple node script that imports terrain.ts via vite-node? Simpler: `node --experimental-strip-types`? Node 26 natively supports type stripping! `node src/terrain.ts`? But the \"babylon-lite\" import resolves via node_modules symlink → package.json main → src/index.ts → Node can't import .ts directly... Node 26 type stripping works for .ts files, but the entry must be a .ts file; a bare specifier \"babylon-lite\" resolves to main: ./src/index.ts — Node will try to load it as JS (package.json doesn't declare a type for the .ts extension... the package is \"type\": \"module\", and Node 26 strips types from .ts). This might actually work. Let me try a small smoke test: import createTerrain from terrain.ts... but createTerrain needs an engine (GPU). However, sampleHeight/carveCrater/raycast can be tested without GPU — they're closures that don't touch the engine, except for the chunk-update calls in carveCrater (updateMeshPositions needs a device).\n\nBetter approach: duplicate the pure math in a test harness and test it? No — that tests a copy, not the code.\n\nAlternative: use vite's SSR module loading: `vite build --ssr`? Overkill.\n\nPractical approach: use vite's dev server itself to smoke-test pure functions — add a small page? I already have `window.__quake`. Without a browser I can't execute JS in a browser context... unless I use jsdom + webgpu mock — too heavy.\n\nWhat I can verify without a browser:\n- Module graph resolution (done: build passes).\n- Math correctness via a Node harness that imports terrain.ts with a mocked babylon-lite module. I can use Node's loader hooks... simpler: temporarily create a node test that imports a re-exported pure function? terrain.ts doesn't export the pure parts separately.\n\nRealistic path: the math in terrain.ts (sampleHeight bilinear, carveCrater, raycast bisection) is simple and I've reviewed it. The riskiest part is engine integration (API names/signatures) — the build passing confirms the exports exist; runtime behavior (rendering, shadows, parenting) I can't verify without a browser, so I'll be honest about that in the summary.\n\nActually — one more meaningful check: run `tsc --noEmit` on just my src (skipLibCheck, with moduleResolution bundler). This will catch signature mismatches (like the 11-arg rayAabb issue that esbuild missed!). tsc will pull in babylon-lite's source TS via imports — will it check that too? No: tsc checks imported files as part of the program... it will type-check babylon-lite's source as well (since it's part of the program via imports). That might surface engine-side errors — but the engine's own repo presumably passes tsc in CI, so errors should be limited to my code. Worth trying: `pnpm exec tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`. If engine-side noise appears, I'll filter to src/ files.\n","seq":17724,"t":8589656,"text":"\n\nBuild is green. Running `tsc` for signature-level checks (this catches what esbuild's build won't):\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":147456,"draft_n":2870,"draft_n_accepted":570,"predicted_ms":27750.0,"predicted_n":980,"prompt_ms":320.0,"prompt_n":52},"to_request":17350}
